# Copy this file to android/key.properties and fill in your own values.
#
# android/.gitignore already excludes key.properties, *.jks and *.keystore,
# so none of this reaches the repository. Keep a backup of the .jks somewhere
# that is not only your laptop.
#
# Create the keystore once. See docs/release_signing.md for the full
# procedure, including why 10,000 days and what happens if you lose the key.
#
#   keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore ~/keystores/stocksafe-upload.p12 \
#     -storetype PKCS12 -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000 \
#     -alias stocksafe
#
# PKCS12, not JKS: keytool warns that JKS is a proprietary format and tells you
# to migrate, so there is no reason to start on the one that prints a warning.
#
# storeFile is resolved relative to the android/ directory, so an absolute path
# outside the repo is the safest choice. Use forward slashes, including on
# Windows — Gradle reads this as a properties file, where a backslash escapes
# the character after it.
#
# keyPassword must be IDENTICAL to storePassword. A PKCS12 keystore has no
# separate key password; keytool forces the two to match, but Gradle still
# reads both fields, so leaving them different fails at signing time with an
# error that does not say why.

# This file is for SIGNING ONLY. Gradle reads exactly four keys from it:
# storeFile, storePassword, keyAlias and keyPassword. Anything else here is
# silently ignored.
#
# In particular STOCKSAFE_VOUCHER_SECRET does NOT belong here. It is a
# String.fromEnvironment resolved at compile time, so it only reaches the app
# through --dart-define on the build command. Putting it in this file produces
# a build that looks correct and ships the development placeholder, in which
# every voucher is forgeable. Use tool/build_release.ps1, which passes it for
# you from C:\keys\stocksafe-voucher-secret.txt.

storeFile=C:/keys/stocksafe-upload.p12
storePassword=
keyAlias=stocksafe
keyPassword=
